  "id": "pike-push-up",
  "name": "Floor Pike Push-Up",
  "definition": "A strict bilateral floor-supported closed-chain bodyweight shoulder-oriented press with hands and feet on the floor and the hips held flexed in an inverted V. NASM establishes the floor pike topology, slightly-wide hands, approximately 45-degree elbow path, head-near-floor endpoint and return short of elbow lockout. The shoulder/scapular actions and existing vertical-press mover hierarchy are transparent anatomy-and-mechanics transfer from Ackland and Seth, not pike-specific motion capture or EMG. Anterior deltoid is primary; triceps, scapular rotators and humeral elevators are secondary; clavicular pectoralis receives bounded secondary shoulder-flexion credit. NASM does not distinguish pectoralis regions, so no measured regional preference or comparative chest/shoulder loading is claimed. Hand/wrist and trunk stabilizer roles are categorical palm-support and posture-control inference. Load is non-comparable with no invented bodyweight percentage. Ordinary, decline, elevated-foot, wall-supported, freestanding, deficit, kipping and dynamic-hip pike variants remain outside. Gluteus maximus and vasti control the held lower-limb posture; gastrocnemius and soleus control the floor-supported ankle/foot. These are categorical support-mechanics inferences, not exact pike measurements.",
